23:03:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: In a multiplayer first‑person shooter, tracking bullets in real time is a delicate balance between immediate feedback for the shooter and a fair, consistent world for everyone. Most modern games use an **authoritative server model**, meaning the server is the ultimate arbiter of what happened, while the client shows you a predicted version to eliminate perceived delay. The method depends on whether the weapon is **hitscan** (instant laser‑like) or **projectile‑based** (travel time). ### 1. Hitscan Weapons (e.g., rifles, pistols) - **What happens on your screen:** When you pull the trigger, your game immediately draws a bullet tracer and shows a hit marker (if you’re aiming at an enemy). This is *client‑side prediction* – it feels instantaneous. - **What happens on the server:** Your game sends a fire‑event to the server, stamped with the exact timestamp of your shot. The server then performs a **raycast** from your weapon’s barrel or camera forward, but using the game state *as it was at that timestamp*. This is called **backwards reconciliation / lag compensation**. The server rewinds all player positions to where they were at your shot time, runs the raycast, and decides if you genuinely hit someone. If you did, damage is applied; if not, the hit marker on your side was a false positive and is corrected silently. This compensates for your internet latency – so aiming at an enemy on your screen means a hit, even if they’ve moved on the server. ### 2. Projectile Weapons (e.g., rockets, arrows) - **Spawning the projectile:** The server immediately spawns the projectile at your authenticated barrel position and gives it an initial velocity. Some games also spawn a cosmetic projectile on your client instantly, but the *authoritative* one is on the server. - **Simulation:** The server updates the projectile’s position every physics tick (gravity, drag, bounce). It checks for collisions with the environment or player hitboxes. Because the server runs the simulation, all players see the same trajectory. - **Client‑side representation:** Your client might also simulate a local copy and show its travel, but the final hit or miss is always decided by the server. If your local projectile appears to hit someone, but the server says it missed (due to latency or minor differences), the client will override its own version. ### 3. How online players see it - **Interpolation:** Other players’ positions are smoothed between server updates, so their movements look fluid. - **Compensation for ping:** The shooter’s aim is always evaluated with lag compensation, so you can lead a target naturally without artificial delay. For projectiles, you have to lead because the travel time is real. ### 4. Real‑time performance All of this happens within **tick rates** (server updates per second, commonly 20–128 Hz). High tick rates mean more frequent raycasts / physics steps, making interactions more precise. The server processes thousands of shots per second, re‑simulating only the tiny slice of the world around each player. **In short:** the game uses a combination of **client prediction** for instant visual feedback, **server‑side hit detection with time‑rewinding** to validate shots fairly, and a **single authoritative simulation** that keeps every online player in the same virtual world – even with latency. This is why you can shoot an enemy on your screen and see them take damage, while the server quietly ensures nobody can cheat or suffer from positional unfairness.
