23:05:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Exactly. The core requirement of *any* shooter – no matter how simple, cheap, or casual – is that when you pull the trigger, the game must determine what the bullet (or beam, or fireball) hits. That inherently means it must track some form of trajectory. Here’s how it holds true across every level of complexity: ### 1. All shooters use one of the two fundamental methods - **Instant hitscan:** A ray is cast from the weapon’s muzzle/camera straight ahead, and the first thing it intersects is the target. - **Simulated projectile:** A game object is spawned with a velocity, moved each frame, and collision‑tested against the world. Cheap mobile titles or simple arcade games almost always pick hitscan because it’s computationally cheaper and easier to implement. They may not have true physics projectiles, but they still trace a line through the virtual space. ### 2. What can differ is the *fidelity* of the tracking - **Collision shapes:** A premium game might test against detailed per‑bone hitboxes; a low‑budget title might just check if the ray intersected a cylinder or even a single bounding sphere around the enemy. - **Continuous collision detection (CCD):** Fast projectiles in AAA games use CCD sweeps to avoid tunneling through thin objects. Simpler games might skip this – so a very fast bullet could occasionally pass through a thin wall without registering. - **Layering and precision:** A high‑end FPS tests the trajectory against all world geometry, destructible glass, different materials, etc. A cheap mobile game might just use a simple distance check against a list of enemies, ignoring level geometry entirely (e.g., line‑of‑sight raycast only for hitscan, no real physics interaction with walls). But even in those minimalist cases, the *trajectory* is still being evaluated – it’s just a more basic version. ### 3. Visuals can be deceptive You might see a glowing bullet fly across the screen in a mobile game and assume it’s a simulated projectile, when in reality the game used an instant hitscan and the “bullet” is purely a cosmetic effect. That still required a trajectory check – just one that happened instantly rather than moving visibly over time. So the underlying logic is the same family. ### 4. Why this is universal The defining feature of a shooter is ranged weapon impact. The only way to decide if a shot connects is to model the path from the attacker to the target. Even the simplest arcade shooter has to perform: ``` target = Raycast(weaponOrigin, aimDirection, maxRange) if target is enemy: damage ``` This is the grain of truth: **every shooter tracks a trajectory** – no matter the budget or era. So yes, you’ve nailed a fundamental axiom of game design. From Doom to a 2D mobile sidescroller with shooting, the same principle holds: the game is always, in some form, tracing a line or moving a point through space to decide what you hit.
